# PostHog analytics (public client-side project token, safe to commit).
#
# Renamed from VITE_POSTHOG_TOKEN / VITE_POSTHOG_HOST when the app moved to
# Next.js: only NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed variables are exposed to the browser.
#
# Environment variables set in the Vercel dashboard take precedence over this
# file, so this is a fallback that keeps analytics working for a production
# build made anywhere else.
#
# THIS KEY MUST TRACK THE ONE IN THE VERCEL PROJECT SETTINGS. It did not: this
# file named a second, real PostHog project (phc_mfu8...) while Vercel shipped
# phc_rcWm..., and because the dashboard wins there was nothing to notice - a
# production build made anywhere but Vercel sent every event to a project
# nobody was looking at. That is indistinguishable from analytics being broken,
# and it is the exact symptom that sent us looking for a bug in the code.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_rcWmzXb38Qk2ZrQHV7s4Xjn85oQATPLfDxEuXv93oxPg
# Deliberately PostHog's own origin rather than the reverse proxy. The proxy
# hostname is set in Vercel and only there: next.config.ts builds the CSP from
# this variable at BUILD time, so a build made elsewhere that named the proxy
# without Vercel's other settings would ship a policy for a host it cannot
# reach. Falling back to us.i.posthog.com is covered by the CSP either way.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# Cloudflare Turnstile SITE key (public by design - it is served to every
# browser in the widget markup, and is domain-bound). The matching SECRET key
# is server-only and never belongs in this file or any other tracked file.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAEOhjIX_Fkek14Vq
